 Yes, you can set up a validation rule in JIRA to prevent an issue from transitioning to the next column if all items on a checklist are not marked as done. This can be achieved using Jira's Conditions and Validators add-on or by writing a custom script using Jira's REST API.

Here's a high-level overview of how to create this validation rule using the Conditions and Validators add-on:

1. Install the Conditions and Validators add-on in your Jira instance.
2. Create a new condition called "Checklist Items Done" or similar.
3. Write a Groovy script for this condition that checks whether all items on the checklist have been completed. The script might look something like this:

```groovy
import com.atlassian.jira.component.ComponentAccessor
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.issues.CustomFieldManager
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.fields.CustomField
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.fields.customfieldtypes.checklist.ChecklistItem

def checklistCustomField = CustomFieldManager.getCustomFieldByNameOrId("customfield_10005", issue) // Replace with your custom field name or ID
if (!checklistCustomField) {
    return []
}

def checklistValues = checklistCustomField.values[0].value

if (checklistValues && checklistValues.size() > 0) {
    def itemsDone = []
    checklistValues.each { checklistValue ->
        def item = ChecklistItem.getChecklistItemObject(issue, checklistValue.id)
        if (item.status == 'done') {
            itemsDone << checklistValue.id
        }
    }

    def itemsNotDone = checklistValues.collect { checklistValue ->
        checklistValue.id
    } - itemsDone

    if (itemsNotDone.size() > 0) {
        return ["message": "Please mark all checklist items as done before transitioning to the next status."]
    }
}

return []
```

