 To give your circle element a more 3D appearance, you can add some additional CSS properties using the `transform` and `perspective` functions. Here's an example of how you could modify the `.circle` class:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
  width: 100px; /* Set a fixed width */
  height: 100px; /* Set a fixed height */
  position: relative;
  perspective: 500px; /* Add perspective */
  transform-style: preserve-3d; /* Preserve 3D transforms */
}

.circle:before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  border-radius: inherit; /* Inherit circle shape */
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc); /* Same background as circle */
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.2), inset 0 0 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.2), inset 0 -5px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.2); /* Add inner shadows for depth effect */
  transform: translateZ(10px); /* Move inner shadow back for depth */
}
```

